 ## Overview
-Uniform Ray Sampling generates a fixed number of sample points along camera rays by uniformly distributing depths between a near and far clipping plane [S4, unofficial][S5][S6, unofficial]. It assumes every point along the ray contributes equally to the final rendering, implying a constant probability density function (PDF) and linearly decaying transmittance [S1]. This method serves as the baseline for volume rendering when no transmittance estimator or spatial skipping structure is available [S1]. It is distinguished from its siblings by its simplicity and lack of scene-awareness; unlike [Stochastic Jittering](924-stochastic-jittering.md) which adds noise to break symmetry, or [Hierarchical Sampling](973-hierarchical-sampling-inverse-cdf-sampling.md) which concentrates samples in high-density regions, uniform sampling treats all intervals equally [S1]. It is computationally efficient to implement but yields the lowest rendering efficiency in most cases compared to spatial skipping or PDF-based methods [S1].
